## 4. Validation and analysis protocol

The source validates the computed structure against the crystallographic bidentate nitrate arrangement and selected X-ray parameters, especially the nitrate bite angle and approximately equivalent Ir-O bonds. It validates the spectrum by comparing nitrate-associated calculated bands with the experimental KBr bands and assigning higher-frequency bands to asymmetric NO2 stretching and lower-frequency bands to symmetric NO2 stretching. The interpretation is comparative, not an assertion that gas-phase harmonic frequencies exactly reproduce the solid-state spectrum.

## 5. Private reference results

The source reports experimental Ir-O distances 2.173(2), 2.174(2) Angstrom; N-O distances 1.294(4), 1.291(4), 1.209(4) Angstrom; O(1)-Ir-O(2) 60.02(9) degrees; O-N-O angles 122.9(3), 122.6(3), 114.5(3) degrees; and nitrate IR bands 1561, 1532, 1261, 1223 cm-1. The DFT table reports Ir-O 2.181, 2.182 Angstrom; N-O 1.354, 1.352, 1.242 Angstrom; O-Ir-O 61.635 degrees; O-N-O 124.397, 124.231, 111.372 degrees; and a calculated nitrate band at 1541 cm-1. The main text also reports calculated nitrate vibrations at 1541, 1102, and 926 cm-1.

## 6. Limitations and interpretation boundaries

The source does not provide a machine-readable Gaussian input or a universal frequency-scaling value in the extracted text. Conformer choice, optimization settings, relativistic treatment, and scaling choices can affect quantitative agreement. Numerical comparisons should therefore be treated as approximate, while structural identity, stationary-point validation, vibrational assignment, and limitations remain essential.
